select m.id, 1, $L$GraphQL vs REST$L$, $L$13 minute read$L$, $L$GraphQL takes a very different shape to REST. Before you test one, you need to understand how a single endpoint and a query language change everything about how you send requests and read responses.$L$, array[$L$Explain how GraphQL differs from REST$L$, $L$Describe the single-endpoint, query-language model$L$, $L$Recognise why GraphQL usually returns HTTP 200$L$], $L$## One endpoint, not many
REST spreads data across many resource URLs (`/users`, `/users/42/orders`). GraphQL exposes **one endpoint** — almost always `POST /graphql` — and you describe exactly what you want in the request body using a **query language**.

## You ask for the shape you want
With REST the server decides the response shape. With GraphQL the client asks for precise fields, so you avoid over-fetching (too much data) and under-fetching (multiple round trips). One query can pull a user and their orders together.

## The schema is the contract
Every GraphQL API publishes a strongly-typed **schema** listing types, fields, queries and mutations. The schema *is* the contract — it tells you what is queryable before you send a thing.

## The trap for testers
Because everything goes through one endpoint, GraphQL usually replies with **HTTP 200 even when something failed**. Errors live in an `errors` array in the JSON body, not in the status code. Asserting on status alone is the single biggest GraphQL testing mistake — we return to it all module.$L$, $L$Against `countries.trevorblades.com` (a public GraphQL API), send `POST /graphql` with the body:

```graphql
query {
  country(code: "GB") {
    name
    capital
    currency
  }
}
```

The response is `200 OK` with `{ "data": { "country": { "name": "United Kingdom", "capital": "London", "currency": "GBP" } } }`. Note: you asked for three fields, you got exactly three — no more, no less.$L$, $L$- Expecting many URLs — GraphQL has one endpoint and a body-driven query
- Assuming a non-200 status signals failure the way REST does
- Thinking GraphQL "replaces" REST rather than being a different trade-off$L$, $L$The first thing to learn on any GraphQL API is where its schema and docs live (often an in-browser explorer at the `/graphql` URL). The schema tells you every legal query before you write one.$L$, $L$Open `https://countries.trevorblades.com` in a browser (it serves a GraphQL explorer). Send a query asking for the `name` and `emoji` of the country with code `"FR"`. Note the HTTP status and where the data appears in the body.$L$, $L$In GraphQL, who decides the shape of the response — the client or the server?$L$, $L$In GraphQL, how many endpoints does a typical API expose for its queries and mutations? (Answer: one — usually POST /graphql)$L$, array[$L$I can explain how GraphQL differs from REST$L$, $L$I understand the single-endpoint model$L$, $L$I know errors can hide behind a 200$L$]
